const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How wide should a front walkway be?',
    a: 'Three feet is the practical minimum for one person, and four feet lets two people walk side by side. We measure your lot and recommend a width that fits the entrance and the frontage.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you curve a concrete walkway?',
    a: 'Yes. Concrete forms can be set to gentle curves, which often suits front yards better than a straight run. We lay out the line with you on site before any forms go in.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What finish is safest in winter?',
    a: 'A broom finish. The fine texture gives grip under snow and ice, which is why we recommend it for walkways. Smooth trowel finishes look sleek but get slippery when wet.',
  },
  {
    q: 'My walkway has heaved. Repair or replace?',
    a: 'If one section has lifted at a joint, a targeted repair can work. If several sections are out of level or the surface is crumbling along its length, replacement gives you a walkway that stays even. We will tell you honestly which one yours needs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a walkway job take?',
    a: 'A typical front walkway is a one to two day job once we start: excavation and base on day one, pour and finish shortly after. We confirm the schedule in writing before work begins, and we stick to it.',
  },
];
